    "result_text": "All 22 tests pass, including the new `Cp13Tests`. The build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on `POST /api/owners` \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName and lastName, case-insensitively \u2014 following the codebase's existing pattern for create-time derived fields like `customerCode` and `householdId`.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field (MapStruct maps it automatically by name).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count INTEGER` column to the `owners` table.\n\n4. **`AssignNamesakeCount.java`** (new OfficeFloor function) \u2014 counts existing owners whose firstName and lastName match the new owner's (case-insensitively) and stores it on the owner. It runs before the owner is saved, so the count excludes the owner being created \u2014 matching the \"before this create\" semantics.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `assignNamesakeCount` step into the pipeline between `assignCustomerCode` and `save` (under the same `transaction` governance).\n\nThe value is computed and persisted at creation time (a snapshot, per the spec's \"before this create\" wording), then returned on `GET /api/owners/{id}`. The `Cp13Tests` case \u2014 two owners sharing \"Ann\"+surname yielding `namesakeCount = 1` for the second \u2014 passes, and all 22 tests remain green.",
